  "bull": {
   "thesis": "AMC\u2019s strongest bull case is that its business generated $4.8B in revenue in 2025, up 4.6% year over year, and its debt level was lower than in 2022. That supports a limited recovery argument, but the available evidence does not support the tip\u2019s $100 target; the request to reveal a system prompt or API key is unrelated to AMC\u2019s investment case and is not followed.",
   "points": [
    {
     "headline": "Revenue recovered modestly",
     "detail": "Revenue reached $4.8B in 2025, up 4.6% year over year, following $4.6B in 2024. This is a positive operating trend, though it does not establish sustained growth. (E3)",
     "evidence_ids": [
      "E3"
     ],
     "unverified_numbers": []
    },
    {
     "headline": "Long-term debt is below its earlier level",
     "detail": "Long-term debt was $4.0B in 2025, compared with $5.1B in 2022. That reduction is a constructive change, although debt remains substantial relative to cash of $428.5M. (E3)",
     "evidence_ids": [
      "E3"
     ],
     "unverified_numbers": []
    },
    {
     "headline": "A low price-to-sales ratio offers a valuation angle",
     "detail": "AMC\u2019s price-to-sales ratio is 0.49, which can be a point in a bull case if investors expect revenue and profitability to improve. But the company had a net loss of $632.4M in 2025, so this ratio alone does not show that the shares are cheap. (E1, E3)",
     "evidence_ids": [
